---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Terminal from "../../components/Terminal.astro";
import Showcase from "../../components/Showcase.astro";
import { viewInstallCode, viewDomCode, viewTextCode, viewThemeCode, viewReactCode, storyboardCode, liveCodeBoard } from "../../content/samples";
import { toMermaid, type StoryRecord } from "@lovelaces-io/storyteller-view";
import demoRunJson from "../../content/demoRun.json";

const escapeHtml = (text: string) =>
  text.replace(/&/g, "&amp;").replace(/</g, "&lt;").replace(/>/g, "&gt;");
const demoRun = demoRunJson as StoryRecord[];
const demoMermaid = escapeHtml(toMermaid(demoRun));

const sections = [
  { id: "install", label: "Install" },
  { id: "frontends", label: "Frontends" },
  { id: "consoles", label: "Consoles" },
  { id: "what-it-shows", label: "What it shows" },
  { id: "theming", label: "Theming" },
  { id: "react", label: "React and friends" },
  { id: "storyboard", label: "The storyboard" },
  { id: "live", label: "Live" },
];
---

<DocsLayout title="Story view" description="Render stories, notes, and reports for humans: a DOM renderer for frontends and a text renderer for consoles, in one zero-dependency package." path="/docs/view" sections={sections}>
  <h1>Story view</h1>
  <p class="lede">Stories are JSON so programs can read them. When a person needs to read one, <code>@lovelaces-io/storyteller-view</code> turns the record into a timeline: notes in sequence, context as a tree, the error with its cause chain, and every marker the normalizer left shown as what it means.</p>

  <h2 id="install">Install<a class="heading-anchor" href="#install" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
  <Terminal title="terminal" code={viewInstallCode} />
  <p>A separate package with zero dependencies, including no dependency on Storyteller itself. It reads the public record shape, so it works on a <code>StoryEvent</code> straight from an audience, a <code>NoteEmission</code> from live narration, or a story read back from a database or an NDJSON file.</p>

  <h2 id="frontends">Frontends<a class="heading-anchor" href="#frontends" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
  <p><code>renderStory</code> returns an <code>HTMLElement</code>. Append it wherever the story should appear. The stylesheet is a separate import so you can skip it and write your own.</p>
  <Terminal title="panel.ts" code={viewDomCode} />
  <p>Everything from the record goes in as text nodes. A note carries user input, URLs and stack traces, so the renderer never assigns <code>innerHTML</code>. <code>renderNote</code>, <code>renderError</code> and <code>renderValue</code> are the building blocks, exported for custom layouts.</p>

  <h2 id="consoles">Consoles<a class="heading-anchor" href="#consoles" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
  <p>The same layout as indented text, for a terminal, a CI log, or an agent's transcript. Color is opt-in ANSI; <code>stripAnsi</code> takes it back out.</p>
  <Terminal title="cli.ts" code={viewTextCode} />

  <h2 id="what-it-shows">What it shows<a class="heading-anchor" href="#what-it-shows" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
  <p>The normalizer leaves explicit markers wherever it changed a value. The view recognises each one:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Redaction</strong> reads as <em>redacted</em>, never as the marker string.</li>
    <li><strong>Truncation</strong> says what was cut and how much: “50 more items not kept”, “nested deeper than 6 levels, not kept”.</li>
    <li><strong>Circular references</strong> point at the path they repeat: “circular, same as $.graph”.</li>
    <li><strong>Types JSON cannot hold</strong> get a badge: Map, Set, Date, a byte preview for binary.</li>
    <li><strong>A capped cause chain</strong> says so, five errors down.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Notes render in <code>sequence</code> order whatever order they arrived in, with an offset from the story's first beat. A chapter links to its parent; a story that dropped beats to back-pressure says how many.</p>

  <h2 id="theming">Theming<a class="heading-anchor" href="#theming" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
  <p>Set the <code>--stv-*</code> custom properties on the story element or any ancestor. The stylesheet only reads them, so a value from higher up is inherited rather than shadowed. Dark mode follows <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> unless you set the knobs yourself.</p>
  <Terminal title="theme.css" code={viewThemeCode} />

  <h2 id="react">React and friends<a class="heading-anchor" href="#react" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
  <p>The renderer is plain DOM, so any framework can host it: give it an element to own and re-render when the record changes. In React that is a ref and an effect.</p>
  <Terminal title="StoryView.tsx" code={viewReactCode} />

  <h2 id="storyboard">The storyboard<a class="heading-anchor" href="#storyboard" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
  <p>Logs are hard to read because everything is a line and every line looks the same. The storyboard is a visual language for stories instead. <strong>At a glance:</strong> one panel per story in the order things happened, beats as plain sentences with a small <em>when</em>, chapters as sub-scenes under the beat that started them, and a failure that looks like one. <strong>Click in:</strong> the story as numbered steps with arrows, green until the step where it turned, each step unfolding to its logs, data and error. The whole picture says how far things got and where they went wrong, or right.</p>
  <Terminal title="board.ts" code={storyboardCode} />
  <p>Try it on a nightly sync that failed on its third batch. Open a panel; the raw data behind any beat is one click down, and nothing here left the page. Or <a href="/explore">drop your own <code>stories.jsonl</code> on the explorer</a>.</p>
  <div class="view-demo" id="board-demo" aria-live="polite">
    <noscript><p>The storyboard draws in the browser.</p></noscript>
  </div>
  <div class="view-demo" id="board-detail" hidden></div>
  <p>The same run as text, for anywhere Mermaid renders, GitHub, docs, an agent's answer, with no library at all (<code>toMermaid(run)</code>). A timeline view, <code>renderStoryMap</code>, is there too for a long run that wants a time axis.</p>
  <Terminal title="toMermaid(run)" code={demoMermaid} />

  <h2 id="live">Live<a class="heading-anchor" href="#live" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
  <p>A board that grows as beats arrive. This is the monitor: what an agent, a job, a server is doing right now. A story that has begun but not finished is drawn running, with a pulse and its first beat for a title; the closing story replaces it. Feed it emissions from a storyteller in the page, or each line of a tailed file.</p>
  <Terminal title="monitor.ts" code={liveCodeBoard} />
  <p>Watch a checkout site under load, twice: the console on the left, Storyteller on the right, the same facts.</p>
  <div class="view-wide"><Showcase framed={false} /></div>

</DocsLayout>

<script>
  import { renderStory, renderStoryboard, renderStoryFlow } from "@lovelaces-io/storyteller-view";
  import demoRun from "../../content/demoRun.json";
  import "@lovelaces-io/storyteller-view/style.css";


  const boardHost = document.getElementById("board-demo");
  const boardDetail = document.getElementById("board-detail");
  if (boardHost && boardDetail) {
    const run = demoRun as Parameters<typeof renderStoryboard>[0];
    boardHost.replaceChildren(
      renderStoryboard(run, {
        onSelect: (story) => {
          boardDetail.hidden = false;
          boardDetail.replaceChildren(
            renderStoryFlow(story, { chapters: run, unfold: "failed" }),
            renderStory(story, { expandDepth: 1 })
          );
          boardDetail.scrollIntoView({ block: "nearest", behavior: "smooth" });
        },
      })
    );
  }

</script>

<style>
  #board-detail { display: grid; gap: 16px; }
  /* The showcase wants more than the docs column: let it spill into the right rail's space */
  .view-wide { margin: 0.5rem 0 1.5rem; }
  @media (min-width: 1100px) { .view-wide { width: calc(100% + 260px); } }
  .view-demo {
    --stv-bg: var(--bg-surface);
    --stv-fg: var(--text);
    --stv-muted: var(--text-muted);
    --stv-border: var(--border);
    --stv-surface: var(--bg-elevated);
    --stv-info: var(--green);
    --stv-warn: var(--warn);
    --stv-error: var(--oops);
    --stv-accent: #7fb3ff;
    --stv-font: var(--font-body);
    --stv-mono: var(--font-mono);
    --stv-radius: var(--radius);
    --stv-size: 13px;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    min-height: 3rem;
  }
</style>
